      "math_academy_id": "q-37070",
      "knowledge_point_id": "231604dc-fe8f-59be-90fe-37af7a1bb648",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying When a Rational Function has no Vertical Asymptotes",
      "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x}{8x-6}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x}{8x-6} & =\\frac{x(4x-3)}{2(4x-3)}=\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987351/assets/3e6ebc8eaec86276442b13fde934234fdaf9c4a457398bb1b792c6910350e44c.png)",
