      "knowledge_point_id": "68ba4c5d-8b91-57bc-b98b-0e9f8d8f1f67",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Vertical Asymptotes for the Sum of Two Rational Functions",
      "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+1} & =\\frac{x+1+(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}=\\frac{2x}{(x-1)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\\,,\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=1$ and $x=-1.$",
      "answer_fields": [
